WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.583
Let’s load an avatar into VR Vlog and get ready to film, step by step.

00:00:08.583 --> 00:00:15.583
This video does not cover buying an avatar or setting up Unity.

00:00:15.583 --> 00:00:24.417
You can find helpful articles in “Prepare to capture” below the video.

00:00:24.417 --> 00:00:29.333
On your computer, open the exporter’s official website.

00:00:29.333 --> 00:00:35.708
Click the “Add to VCC / ALCOM” button.

00:00:35.708 --> 00:00:40.375
If asked to open ALCOM, choose Open.

00:00:40.375 --> 00:00:47.583
On the ALCOM screen, click the button to add the repository.

00:00:47.583 --> 00:00:56.333
Open Projects on the left and click Manage for the project containing your avatar.

00:00:56.333 --> 00:01:06.333
Search for “VR Vlog,” then click the plus button next to the exporter.

00:01:06.333 --> 00:01:14.583
Click Apply. Required packages will be installed with the exporter.

00:01:14.583 --> 00:01:19.292
When installation finishes, click Open Unity.

00:01:19.292 --> 00:01:29.625
In Unity, choose VR Vlog → Export lilToon VRM 1.0 from the top menu.

00:01:29.625 --> 00:01:36.708
Drag the top-level Kipfel object from Hierarchy into the Avatar field.

00:01:36.708 --> 00:01:42.500
Select the parent of the whole avatar, not just its body or clothes.

00:01:42.500 --> 00:01:52.875
Next, enter the creator’s name. For this Kipfel example, enter the model creator’s name, Kameyama.

00:01:52.875 --> 00:01:57.833
Click “Choose destination and export VRM.”

00:01:57.833 --> 00:02:08.917
Choose a folder and file name, then click Save. Here, we use Kipfel.vrm.

00:02:08.917 --> 00:02:18.542
Transfer the exported file to iPhone using a cloud service such as Google Drive or Dropbox.

00:02:18.542 --> 00:02:27.375
You can also connect your iPhone to the computer and transfer it to a file-sharing app.

00:02:27.375 --> 00:02:32.625
This guide uses iCloud Drive.

00:02:32.625 --> 00:02:39.208
In a computer browser, open Drive on iCloud.com.

00:02:39.208 --> 00:02:46.042
Sign in with the same Apple Account you use on your iPhone.

00:02:46.042 --> 00:02:50.292
Click the New Folder button at the top.

00:02:50.292 --> 00:02:55.917
Name it VRVlog and press Enter.

00:02:55.917 --> 00:03:00.875
Double-click the folder you just created.

00:03:00.875 --> 00:03:06.083
Click the upload button with an upward arrow on a cloud.

00:03:06.083 --> 00:03:14.292
Select the Kipfel.vrm file you exported, then click Open.

00:03:14.292 --> 00:03:23.000
Wait for the upload to finish and the file to appear in the folder.

00:03:23.000 --> 00:03:31.500
On your iPhone, open VR Vlog and tap the gear icon on the capture screen.

00:03:31.500 --> 00:03:39.917
In Settings, under Change model, tap “Choose a VRM on this device.”

00:03:39.917 --> 00:03:46.250
In the file picker, tap Browse and open iCloud Drive.

00:03:46.250 --> 00:03:49.500
Open the VRVlog folder.

00:03:49.500 --> 00:03:55.542
Tap Kipfel.vrm inside it.

00:03:55.542 --> 00:04:05.625
If it has a cloud icon, wait for the download. Then wait for the avatar to load.

00:04:05.625 --> 00:04:11.375
When the avatar appears, you are ready to film.

00:04:11.375 --> 00:04:20.375
Narration was provided by Zundamon.
